WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.786
DexCompose reuses pretrained dexterous policies for multi-task manipulation with a single hand.

2
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.330
Idle degrees of freedom within a trained policy are

3
00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.995
a reusable resource for composing additional behaviors.

4
00:00:10.995 --> 00:00:13.545
DexCompose identifies finger-level redundancy through

5
00:00:13.545 --> 00:00:15.819
release tests on successful held-object states.

6
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.772
The frozen-grasp baseline fixes the first skill’s finger

7
00:00:18.772 --> 00:00:21.772
configuration while executing downstream wrist actions.

8
00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:24.866
This is insufficient for reliable composition, motivating

9
00:00:24.866 --> 00:00:27.784
explicit action ownership and residual stabilization.

10
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.955
We evaluate sixteen composite tasks spanning four

11
00:00:30.955 --> 00:00:33.923
object-retention skills and four downstream interactions.

12
00:00:33.923 --> 00:00:37.759
Both pretrained full-hand policies remain frozen during composition.

13
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.773
DexCompose combines training-free finger attribution,

14
00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:44.038
explicit action ownership, and dual residual stabilization.

15
00:00:44.038 --> 00:00:46.726
The two residuals correct the preserved reference and the frozen

16
00:00:46.726 --> 00:00:49.785
downstream policy within their allocated action subspaces.

17
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.005
Release tests evaluate candidate masks from successful post-grasp states.

18
00:00:54.005 --> 00:00:57.782
Preserved fingers replay the reference grasp while released fingers gradually open.

19
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.400
We measure object retention and clean release.

20
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.363
A task-aware allocation agent balances retention stability,

21
00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:05.824
clean release quality, and residual dexterity.

22
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.876
DexCompose achieves seventy-seven point four percent

23
00:01:08.876 --> 00:01:11.770
average composite success across sixteen tasks.

24
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.810
These compositions pair each object-retention skill with door opening.

25
00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:20.980
Task A controls the preserved finger joints, while Task B controls the released fingers and wrist.

26
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.219
The two base policies remain frozen.

27
00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:25.880
Lightweight residuals adapt their composition without

28
00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.807
training a separate full policy for every task pair.

29
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.238
These compositions pair the same four skills with button pressing.

30
00:01:32.238 --> 00:01:36.271
Selecting fingers requires considering their utility for the downstream task.

31
00:01:36.271 --> 00:01:41.280
In the ball-grasping example, the allocation agent chooses a thumb, ring, and little-finger grasp,

32
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.772
keeping the index finger free for subsequent tasks instead of maximizing retention alone.

33
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.293
These compositions use microwave opening as the downstream skill.

34
00:01:49.293 --> 00:01:52.450
The Task-A residual corrects the stored reference configuration.

35
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.426
The Task-B residual corrects the nominal action from the frozen downstream policy.

36
00:01:56.426 --> 00:01:59.798
At every control step, both residuals are recomputed from the latest

37
00:01:59.798 --> 00:02:02.819
observation and applied relative to their respective reference actions.

38
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.169
These compositions use switch actuation as the downstream skill.

39
00:02:06.169 --> 00:02:10.586
Composite success requires completing Task B while preserving Task A throughout its execution.

40
00:02:10.586 --> 00:02:15.219
Across all sixteen tasks, DexCompose achieves the best performance among the evaluated methods.

41
00:02:15.219 --> 00:02:17.487
It improves mean success by eight point two percentage

42
00:02:17.487 --> 00:02:19.843
points over the variant without the Task-B residual.

43
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.549
Removing the Task-A residual causes the most severe degradation.

44
00:02:23.549 --> 00:02:26.596
Task-aware allocation and action masking reduce interference,

45
00:02:26.596 --> 00:02:29.821
while the Task-B residual further improves execution refinement.

46
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.006
Three-skill sequences grasp two objects in succession, then

47
00:02:33.006 --> 00:02:35.998
perform a downstream interaction while retaining both objects.

48
00:02:35.998 --> 00:02:40.660
Release tests and the allocation agent assign disjoint finger masks across the three policies.

49
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.818
Composite success reaches sixty-two point one one percent when sufficient finger redundancy remains.

50
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.435
A real-to-sim-to-real pipeline transfers DexCompose to a Franka arm with a LEAP hand.

51
00:02:50.435 --> 00:02:55.797
Across four composite tasks, it achieves fifty-nine successful executions in eighty physical trials.

52
00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.810
DexCompose composes pretrained dexterous manipulation policies.
